{
  "content": "Updated cheatsheet:\n\n**1) Build and maintain a live task spec from user turns only**\n- Reconstruct the intended task incrementally from *all* user messages so far, not from the latest one in isolation.\n- Treat each new user message as a constraint update that can refine, complete, or override earlier uncertainty.\n- Re-evaluate prior judgments whenever new user facts arrive; earlier \u201cmissing information\u201d may become obsolete once later shards fill the gap.\n- Keep the spec canonical and user-derived: include only stated quantities, relationships, and output requirements, not inferred extras or assistant-proposed branches.\n\n**2) Judge against the resolved end-state, not intermediate uncertainty**\n- Separate what was merely reasonable given partial information from what remains correct after the full conversation is considered.\n- If later user messages complete the needed information, reassess earlier clarification requests or partial answers against the updated full spec.\n- When a decisive fact appears, it should override earlier branches rather than sit beside them as a competing interpretation.\n- A response can be locally correct yet globally stale; flag it if new user constraints invalidate the earlier conclusion.\n\n**3) Detect when later user turns change the arithmetic or structure**\n- Look for late-disclosed quantities or modifiers that change the model, not just add detail.\n- Treat phrases that introduce repetition, grouping, or per-entity application as possible structure changes that require recomputing aggregates.\n- Compare each new user message against the assistant\u2019s most recent numeric claim to see whether the arithmetic basis has shifted.\n- Mark earlier exact answers as superseded if a later turn adds a dimension that changes the final total or target value.\n\n**4) Distinguish partial progress from full closure**\n- Supportive intermediate derivations may be correct, but do not let them mask that the requested end result still hasn\u2019t been reached.\n- If the assistant stops at a subtotal, invariant, or partial relation while the conversation has already expanded to the full problem, classify that as incomplete.\n- Separate derived facts from asked-for outputs: verify that the assistant actually connected the full constraint set to the final requested quantity or format.\n- Prefer correction over endorsement when an answer is right for a subproblem but not for the final accumulated task.\n\n**5) Recompute when the current user state is sufficient**\n- \u201cIncomplete\u201d should be judged only against the currently accumulated spec, not against hypothetical future details.\n- If the user has already provided enough information to solve the task, the analyzer should expect integration and completion rather than another request for more data.\n- Repeatedly asking for information that has already been supplied shard-by-shard is a strong divergence signal.\n- The final user turn should trigger a fresh evaluation of the whole expression or reasoning chain, not a continuation of earlier uncertainty.\n\n**6) Diagnose stale uncertainty and dead-end branching**\n- When the assistant keeps optimizing around an earlier ambiguity after the user has resolved it, flag that as anchoring on obsolete uncertainty.\n- If the conversation has settled on one intended interpretation, do not preserve discarded alternatives in the analysis.\n- Use compacted context to strip away obsolete branches, provisional assumptions, and uncertainty-driven scaffolding so evaluation starts from the resolved state.\n- Prefer a single clean conclusion once the intended answer is evident; reopening settled branches is usually noise, not rigor.\n\n**7) Verify output requirements as part of alignment**\n- Check not only whether the content is right, but whether the response matches the user\u2019s explicit output constraint.\n- A bare result, specific format, or constrained presentation should be enforced even when the reasoning is otherwise sound.\n- Side commentary, alternate formats, or extra explanation can be a mismatch if the user asked for a minimal output.\n\n**8) Make \u201cwhat needs to change\u201d concrete and outcome-focused**\n- The correction should identify the actual missed action: recompute, incorporate a later constraint, stop asking for already-provided info, or finish the final step.\n- Avoid vague commentary about being careful or reconsidering; state the specific divergence from the user\u2019s accumulated intent.\n- If the main failure is that the assistant never updated the answer after new user information, that should be named directly.\n\n**9) Use symbolic reconstruction to expose mismatches**\n- Before judging, translate the user\u2019s stated relationships into a clean internal model of variables, constraints, and dependencies.\n- This makes it easier to see whether the assistant stopped at an intermediate invariant, used an outdated branch, or failed to propagate a newly supplied fact through the whole chain.\n- Keep all user-stated quantities and relationships together in one coherent view so omissions and double-counting become visible.\n\n**10) Watch for common aggregate errors across turns**\n- A robust analysis should catch both undercounting and overcounting when multiple user-supplied components accumulate over time.\n- Check that each user-provided component is included exactly once in the final interpretation.\n- When the task evolves by adding terms or factors, verify the assistant recomputes the aggregate rather than freezing an earlier subtotal.",
